# A snapshot, driven the way a person drives one: a package, a suite beside it,
# a golden picture checked in, and one `buri` command.
#
# The picture is the assertion. `test/__snapshots__/card.png` was recorded by
# `buri test --update` and is compared byte for byte, so this case is also the
# only place that says the renderer produces the same bytes on Linux and on
# macOS — a golden that did not would fail on one of the two CI hosts and pass
# on the other.
#
# Four steps, and the middle two are the ones worth reading.
#
# **Wrapping the tree in an unstyled `stack` changes nothing.** That is the
# proving test of the whole design: a container with no style must be
# transparent to layout, so the same picture comes back. A renderer that laid
# the canvas out differently from a `stack` would pass every other assertion
# here and fail this one, which is what it caught the day it was written.
#
# **And the negative twin the corpus rule asks for.** One word of the tree
# changes, the run fails, the report names the file to look at, and the diff
# image is on disk beside the golden. A snapshot suite that could not fail
# would be a suite that never looked at a pixel.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "a card is painted, compared byte for byte, unchanged by an unstyled stack around it, and fails with a diff when the tree changes"

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/card", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "pass.txt"
}

# Nothing was written: a run that matched leaves no diff image behind.
path { path: "lib/card/test/__snapshots__/card.diff.png"  exists: false }

# The proving test. An unstyled container is transparent to layout, so the
# bytes are the same bytes.
edit {
  file: "lib/card/test/card.buri"
  replace: "snapshot(ctx, \"card\", card(.Const(\"Ada\")), .Hover);"
  with: "snapshot(ctx, \"card\", ui.stack([], [card(.Const(\"Ada\"))]), .Hover);"
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/card", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "wrapped.txt"
}

# The negative twin: one word of the tree, and the report a reader gets.
edit {
  file: "lib/card/test/card.buri"
  replace: ".Const(\"Ada\")"
  with: ".Const(\"Grace\")"
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/card", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "changed.txt"
}

path { path: "lib/card/test/__snapshots__/card.diff.png"  exists: true }

# And the way back: `--update` records the tree as it is now, and the run after
# it compares against what it just wrote.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/card", "--update", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "recorded.txt"
}

path { path: "lib/card/test/__snapshots__/card.diff.png"  exists: false }

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/card", "--force"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "again.txt"
}
